BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 72 740 Td
(ISO 26262: functional safety for road vehicles) '
() '
(ISO 26262 is the international standard for the functional safety of electrical and) '
(electronic systems in series-production road vehicles. It adapts the general standard IEC) '
(61508 to the automotive life cycle, from concept and development to production, operation) '
(and decommissioning.) '
() '
(Hazard analysis and risk assessment \(HARA\) is the starting point. Each hazardous event is) '
(rated for severity \(S0 to S3\), exposure \(E0 to E4\) and controllability \(C0 to C3\). The) '
(combination gives the Automotive Safety Integrity Level, or ASIL.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
5 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 9 0 R >> >> /Contents 6 0 R >>
endobj
6 0 obj
<< /Length 623 >>
stream
BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 72 740 Td
(Automotive Safety Integrity Levels) '
() '
(There are four ASILs, from ASIL A \(lowest\) to ASIL D \(highest\). Hazards that need no) '
(safety measures beyond normal quality management are classified as QM. An airbag or a) '
(braking system is typically ASIL D, while rear lights are often ASIL A or B.) '
() '
(A higher ASIL demands stricter methods: more rigorous requirements tracing, more testing,) '
(higher coverage targets and more independence in reviews. ASIL decomposition allows a) '
(high-ASIL requirement to be split across redundant, independent elements with lower ASILs.) '
ET
endstream
endobj
7 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Resources << /Font << /F1 9 0 R >> >> /Contents 8 0 R >>
